The attached image illustrates the transient inflammatory response induced by Nano-11 at the injection site and its uptake by recruited cells. Panel A shows the kinetics of various immune cells, including neutrophils, Mac-2+ macrophages, antigen-presenting cells, eosinophils, and Ly6C+ monocytes, over 21 days post-injection. Neutrophils and Ly6C+ monocytes peak early (around Day 1-2), followed by other cell types, with inflammation largely resolving by Day 21. Panel B provides confocal microscopy images at different time points, demonstrating that AF647-labeled Nano-11 (red) is efficiently internalized by recruited MHCII+, Ly6C+, Mac-2+, and 1A8+ cells (green) at the injection site, confirming the adjuvant's role in antigen delivery to these immune cells for subsequent transport to draining lymph nodes.